  "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\u27e82,\\,-4,\\,6\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-2,\\,4,\\,-6\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}f(t)-\u27e83,\\,-1,\\,-2\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\u27e83,\\,-1,\\,-2\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \u27e8-2,\\,4,\\,-6\u27e9-{\u27e83t,\\,-t,\\,-2t\u27e9|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\u27e8-1,\\,2,\\,-3\u27e9-\u27e86,\\,-2,\\,-4\u27e9\\text{\u00a0} \\\\  & =\u27e8-7,\\,4,\\,1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
